31.m:
The wind is your movement, O hero, and he is celebrated as Garutmān;
may the goddess Lakṣmī, who traverses the three worlds, always be in your resort.
And may the Dvādaśī be your lunar day, assuming forms at will.

32.m:
Thus addressed, being well-balanced with four feet in the Kṛta yuga,
he stood on three in the Tretā, and became two in the Dvāpara;
in the Kali, the lord protects the subjects with a single foot.

He has six divisions among Brahmins, is established in three ways among the Kṣatriyas,
in two ways among the Vaiśyas, and in one way among the Śūdras—the Lord is all-pervading;
in all the Rasātalas and on the continents and realms, he is the Lord himself.

When he had departed, all the gods, deprived of righteousness,
wishing to seize the wives of the Asuras, wandered to their dwellings;
the Asuras too likewise wandered to the dwellings of the gods.

38.m:
As he arrived there, all-auspicious [women]
came forth from the trees with golden vessels held before them in their hands;
having promptly given him various foods, his vessel was filled to the brim.

The sage said:
“Your former body is gone today, O virtuous one, as you abstained from food;
your body made of asceticism has become separate, without a doubt.

Your former understanding has been destroyed; now know that pure, imperishable one.
You are of a purified body, and your body is now another;
by means of it the Vedas, together with the scriptures, will shine forth to you, O sage.”

39.m:
Durvāsas said:
“Not two, but three bodies are to be stated, not the reverse;
the bodies of living beings are threefold, the abode of distinct experiences.

That third one, which is beyond the senses, is for the experience of righteousness and unrighteousness;
its threefold division is proclaimed by wise knowers of the Brahman:
retribution, righteous experience, and mundane enjoyment—thus it has three divisions.

Now, as you practice auspicious conduct, asceticism, and uprightness,
another body of the nature of righteousness has become established in you;
by means of it you are fit to know the Vedas and the Purāṇas, without doubt.

Durvāsas said:
“The section on ritual action is rooted in knowledge, and knowledge begins with ritual action;
there is no difference between the two, just as between a stone and earth, O sage.

The section on ritual action is taught as fourfold among the brahmins and others;
among them, three [classes] continually perform the actions prescribed by the Veda,
while one performs service to the three; this is the rite ordained by the Veda.

49.m:
I see no other on the surface of the earth except him.
Therefore, thinking ‘O virtuous Agastya!’, I praised myself;
overcome with great joy, O king, I spoke thus.

Born in the lineage of Prajāpati, while still a boy he went to the forest;
having worshipped Viṣṇu, that most splendid abode was attained [by him].
Therefore I said, ‘O virtuous Dhruva!’, O best of kings.

50.m:
With ‘Homage to the thousand-headed one’, one should worship Hari's head;
and his two arms with ‘To Puruṣa’; his throat with ‘To the universal-formed’;
his weapons with ‘To the weapon of knowledge’; and his chest with ‘To Śrīvatsa’.
